@Shimada666 Good observation, I missed that. In that case, it would make sense to use the approach you have implemented, but I can see it may have an issue of its own.

From my understanding, if the message is a long one, then the auto-scroll forces the user to follow the message until it finishes generating (it will "pull down" the user every time the scroll height updates). This is less convenient for longer messages, as users often scroll up to read previous material while the latest ones finish generating.

Actually, if that user behavior is common, it may be preferable to have the scroll behavior as it is now (though we can include a "Scroll to Bottom" action nearby).

We can solve the problem presented above by doing something like listening in with the `ResizeObserver` and remove listener when the user scrolls. But is it worth introducing more complexity for this feature?

@Sparkier WDYT?

@amanape I understand your concerns. I believe that in the current scenario, maintaining the scroll to the bottom is a better approach. Whether it's your implementation or mine, it will trigger automatic scrolling to the bottom when the agent sends a new message.

In fact, a better approach, as you mentioned, would be to introduce a state like autoScroll, similar to chatgpt-next-web, and listen to user scroll events to track the scroll position. When the user scrolls up (i.e., when the scroll height is less than the bottom height), set autoScroll to false. When the user scrolls to the bottom or a new message is sent, assume that the user wants to see the new message and set autoScroll to true. This method works well in chatgpt-next-web.

