I don't think this is the best solution since using the `useQuery` and `useMutation` hooks to manage synchronous comes with too much unnecessary overhead. Calculating cache time, fetch status, etc. and utilizing lifecycle callbacks such as `onError` / `onMutate` for these synchronous changes.

As one alternative, we might be able to avoid this boilerplate entirely and utilize the query client directly to manage this state. For example:

`frontend/src/context/ws-client-provider.tsx`
```ts
function handleMessage(event: Record<string, unknown>) {
  if (isOpenHandsEvent(event) && isStatusChangeEvent(event)) {
    const statusMessage = // get status message from event object
    queryClient.setQueryData(["status", "currentMessage"], statusMessage);
  }
}
```

In turn, `useStatusMessage` will actually be a simple getter
```ts
export const useStatusMessage = () => {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();
  return queryClient.getQueryData(["status", "statusMessage"]);
}
```

With no unnecessary overhead from the internals of `useQuery` / `useMutation`

Yet another, more traditional alternative would be implementing a custom React context:

```ts
import { createContext, useContext, useState, ReactNode } from "react";

type StatusContextType = {
  status: string;
  setStatus: (message: string) => void;
};

const StatusContext = createContext<StatusContextType | undefined>(undefined);

export const StatusProvider = ({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) => {
  const [status, setStatus] = useState("");

  return (
    <StatusContext.Provider value={{ status, setStatus }}>
      {children}
    </StatusContext.Provider>
  );
};

export const useStatus = () => {
  const context = useContext(StatusContext);
  if (!context) {
    throw new Error("useStatus must be used within a StatusProvider");
  }
  return context;
};
```

